Read the builtin geometric types as their Postgres text form.

Upstream maps point to STRUCT(x DOUBLE, y DOUBLE) and line, lseg, box, path,
polygon and circle to LIST(DOUBLE). pg_lake has no Iceberg mapping for these
types and stores them as a string holding the Postgres text form, which is
also what the pg_lake_replication change-log decoder writes for them. A scan
that returns a struct or a list therefore writes Parquet whose schema does not
match the Iceberg column, and the read fails with "Parquet file schema node
type does not match table column".

We annotate these types CAST_TO_VARCHAR at type-resolution time, so the
existing varchar path casts the column server-side (col::VARCHAR, or
::VARCHAR[] for an array of them) and the scanned value is exactly what
Postgres itself prints.

The types are matched by pg_catalog OID rather than by the name upstream
switches on: a user-defined type in its own schema may well be called "point",
and it has to keep resolving as the composite or enum it is.  The type OID
comes from composite-type-resolution.patch, which is applied before this one.

Note: the coordinate-wise struct/list representation is no longer reachable
through the scanner; a caller that wants coordinates has to parse the text.

